In our internal modeling, we applied the loan-limit formula to a $60,000 vested balance with no earlier loans. The limit on all plan loans together was $30,000, leaving $30,000 available for a new loan. For a $10,000 loan at an example 8% APR, the payment was $202.76 per month over 5 years. Total interest was $2,165.84, and total repaid was $12,165.84. Those payment figures use a level-payment schedule. They do not establish the rate your plan would charge. Change the inputs below to see how a different loan amount, rate or payment schedule changes the result. $30,000 largest new loan at $60,000 vested $202.76 payment on $10,000 over 5 years $2,166 total interest Share Interactive — edit any field How the Loan Limit Is Calculated Under 26 U.S.C. 72(p)(2)(A) , the limit on all plan loans together is the smaller of two amounts. The first starts at $50,000 and falls by the amount your highest plan-loan balance in the past 12 months exceeded what you owe today. The second is the greater of half your vested balance or $10,000. The calculator compares those two amounts, then subtracts Plan loans you owe now to show room for a new loan. If you have borrowed before, the limit on all loans is not the amount you can take out today. Enter the highest balance even if you have since paid down that loan, because the earlier balance can reduce the $50,000 prong. Vested balance Owed now 12-month peak Limit on all loans Room for a new loan $15,000 None None $10,000 $10,000 $60,000 None None $30,000 $30,000 $100,000 None None $50,000 $50,000 $200,000 None None $50,000 $50,000 $200,000 $10,000 $20,000 $40,000 $30,000 $200,000 $20,000 $50,000 $20,000 $0 The $15,000 balance produces a $10,000 limit because half the vested balance is $7,500 and the statutory floor is higher. A plan may also cap a loan at your vested balance. At a $100,000 vested balance with nothing owed, half the balance reaches the $50,000 cap. A higher vested balance does not raise that cap: the $200,000 example with no loans also has a $50,000 limit. The prior-loan example shows why the two subtractions must stay separate. With $10,000 owed and a $20,000 peak, the peak exceeds today's balance by $10,000, reducing the $50,000 prong to $40,000. The calculator then subtracts the $10,000 still owed from the $40,000 limit, leaving $30,000 for a new loan. Subtracting the $10,000 a second time would count it twice and wrongly leave $20,000 of room. In the last table row, the peak reduces that prong to $20,000, which equals the amount already owed. The result is no room for another loan under the federal cap, even though the vested balance is higher. Repayment Rules the Calculator Checks A general-purpose plan loan must be repayable within 5 years under 26 U.S.C. 72(p)(2)(B) and (C) . The statute makes an exception for a loan used to buy a dwelling that will become the participant's main home within a reasonable time. It also requires substantially level payments at least quarterly. Choose General purpose or Buy a main home under Loan purpose so the calculator can check the term you entered. It warns when a general-purpose term exceeds 5 years or when Payments per year is less often than quarterly. A green note means the entered amount, term and payment frequency fit the federal rules the calculator checks. It does not mean your plan offers loans. The calculator also warns when Loan amount you want exceeds Largest new loan allowed. Under 26 U.S.C. 72(p)(1) , the portion outside the qualifying loan rules is treated as a distribution. Use the 401(k) early withdrawal calculator for a tax estimate rather than treating that excess as an ordinary loan payment. For payments, the calculator divides APR by Payments per year to get the periodic rate. It multiplies Term (years) by Payments per year to get the number of payments, then applies payment = P x r / (1 - (1 + r)^-n). Each payment covers that period's interest. The remainder reduces the loan balance. Example rate Monthly payment on $10,000 Term Total interest 6% $193.33 5 years $1,599.68 8% $202.76 5 years $2,165.84 10% $212.47 5 years $2,748.23 These rates are examples to replace with the rate in your plan terms. Payment frequency changes the schedule too: at an example 8%, the same $10,000 loan over 5 years has 20 quarterly payments of $611.57 each. At that rate, paying every two weeks produces 130 payments of $93.45 each. A longer permitted main-home term can lower each payment while increasing total interest in the supplied example. A $30,000 loan at an example 8% costs $608.29 per month over 5 years, with $6,497.51 in total interest. Over 15 years, the main-home example costs $286.70 per month, with $21,605.21 in total interest. Run your own amount and rate before relying on any example payment. The federal limit and repayment rules determine whether the amount qualifies as a loan rather than a distribution, while the plan document decides whether borrowing is available at all. If the plan document has no loan provision, this calculator cannot make a loan available. Borrowing from a Solo 401(k) The same federal loan-limit formula applies to an owner-only 401(k), so a solo 401k loan calculator needs no separate limit setting. The plan document must provide for loans, and a provision alone does not establish that a participant loan avoids a prohibited transaction under 26 U.S.C. 4975(d)(1) . An owner is generally a key employee under 26 U.S.C. 416(i) , so interest on the owner's own plan loan is not deductible for any period the owner is a key employee ( 26 U.S.C. 72(p)(3) ). Read the solo section of the 401(k) loan rules guide , then enter your vested balance in the calculator above. What the Calculator Does Not Include The calculator does not include plan fees or determine your plan's interest rate or loan limits. Enter the interest rate and loan amount you want in the calculator. The calculator does not estimate income tax or additional tax if a loan is treated as a distribution. Use the 401(k) early withdrawal calculator for a tax estimate, and ask a tax professional about a missed payment. The calculator assigns no market return to money while it is out of the plan and does not determine where repayments are credited. Account for the borrowed money's time out of the market separately. Whether interest you repay is credited to your account depends on the plan document. The calculator does not compare a plan loan with another borrowing source. Use the 401(k) loan vs home equity line of credit (HELOC) comparison to weigh the borrowing choices. Frequently asked questions How are 401k loan amounts calculated? The federal limit on all your plan loans is the smaller of a reduced $50,000 cap or the greater of half your vested balance and $10,000. The cap falls by any amount your highest plan-loan balance in the past 12 months exceeded what you owe today. Subtract loans you owe now from the resulting limit to find room for a new loan. With $60,000 vested and no earlier loans, the limit and room for a new loan are both $30,000. How much would a $10,000 401(k) loan cost per month over 5 years? A $10,000 loan paid monthly over 5 years costs $202.76 per month at an example 8% APR. At example rates of 6% and 10%, the payments are $193.33 and $212.47 per month, respectively. These are example rates. A plan sets its own rate. Enter the interest rate from your plan terms in this calculator to see your own payment. How many 401(k) loans can you have at once? The federal loan-limit text states no maximum number of simultaneous plan loans. It counts every outstanding plan loan toward the limit. Your plan document decides whether loans are available and whether it limits their number. Enter all Plan loans you owe now to calculate the room left for another loan. Can I take a loan from a solo 401(k)? A solo 401(k) can provide for participant loans, but its plan document must allow them. The same federal loan-limit formula applies to an owner-only plan, and a plan provision alone does not settle the prohibited-transaction requirements. Read the solo section of the 401(k) loan rules guide before using the calculator for a proposed loan. What happens if I do not repay a 401(k) loan? A plan loan that fails to meet the qualifying loan rules can be treated as a distribution under 26 U.S.C. 72(p)(1). The calculator does not determine the tax consequences of a missed payment. Ask a tax professional to confirm those consequences, and use the 401(k) early withdrawal calculator for a tax estimate. Your plan document determines whether loans are offered, the interest rate, fees and any limit on the number of loans. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . 26 U.S.C. 72(p): Loans treated as distributions (Cornell Legal Information Institute) 26 U.S.C. 4975: Tax on prohibited transactions (Cornell Legal Information Institute) 26 U.S.C. 416(i): Key employee definition (Cornell Legal Information Institute) Related retirement calculators 401k Early Withdrawal Calculator Free · instant Retirement Savings Calculator Free · instant RMD Calculator Free · instant See all retirement calculators , or browse every ModernWallet calculator . Plan the bigger picture When does waiting to claim Social Security pay off? → Roth or Traditional 401(k)? Compare after tax → Grow your investments → Pitched an IUL? Run the numbers → Track your net worth → Check your asset mix → Could you stop saving already? → Thinking about retiring early? → Is $3 million enough? → Shelter money from RMDs with a QLAC → ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results. 📣 Advertise With Us Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Bu